    "https://jobs.okstate.edu/jobs/faculty-researcher-biology-department-lu-001539-langston-oklahoma-united-states": {
      "title": "Faculty Researcher Biology Department LU-001539",
      "description": "Faculty Researcher Biology Department LU-001539 - Langston, Oklahoma, United States Faculty Researcher Biology Department LU-001539 - Langston, Oklahoma, United States Kiosk mode Faculty Researcher Biology Department LU-001539 493246 Langston VP ACADEMIC AFFAIRS (LU) Faculty PhD Campus LANGSTON Contact Name & Email Nika Wilson , luhumanresources@langston.edu Work Schedule Monday through Friday, 8:00am-5:00pm with occasional evenings and weekends. Appointment Length 10 months Hiring Range $70000 - $75000 Commensurate with education and experience Priority Application Date While applications will be accepted until a successful candidate has been hired, interested parties are encouraged to submit their materials by to ensure full consideration. Special Instructions to Applicants For full consideration, please include a resume, cover letter, official transcript(s) and three professional letters of recommendation. About this Position The Biology Department at Langston University invites applications for a assistant professor to begin in the upcoming academic year. We're seeking a dynamic educator and researcher to join our vibrant academic community. This position offers a unique opportunity to blend teaching and cutting-edge research. The successful candidate will be responsible for teaching undergraduate courses in Anatomy and Zoology . In addition to teaching, the faculty member will conduct research within our state-of-the-art Science Research Institute . A key role will be to mentor, train, and guide undergraduate students through hands-on biomedical research projects. The candidate will also have the support and opportunity to develop and establish their own independent research program. Required Qualifications Ph.D./Ed.D. Biology or closely related field (degree must be conferred on or before agreed upon start date) A minimum of one year of postdoctoral research experience is strongly preferred, reflecting a demonstrated ability to conduct independent scholarly inquiry and contribute meaningfully to the academic community Skills, Proficiencies, and/or Knowledge: Pedagogical Skills: Demonstrated ability to effectively teach and engage undergraduate students in both lecture and laboratory settings. This includes curriculum development, lesson planning, and assessment of student learning. Mentorship and Advising: Skill in guiding, training, and inspiring undergraduate students in a research environment, fostering their intellectual and professional growth. Grant Writing: Proven ability to write clear, persuasive, and successful grant proposals to secure funding from agencies like the NIH, NSF, or private foundations. Communication Skills: Excellent written and oral communication skills for delivering lectures, publishing research in peer-reviewed journals, and presenting at scientific conferences. Interpersonal Skills: Strong ability to collaborate effectively with faculty, staff, and students and contribute positively to the departmental community. Laboratory Management: Competence in managing a research lab, including overseeing lab safety, ordering supplies, maintaining equipment, and managing a budget. Preferred Qualifications Ph.D./Ed.D. The most competitive candidates will likely have a research focus in areas that naturally integrate the teaching and research duties. Ideal fields of study include: Developmental Biology: This field is an excellent fit as it inherently connects anatomy, genetics, and molecular mechanisms during an organism's growth. Comparative Physiology: This specialization directly links anatomical structures (Anatomy) across different animal groups (Zoology) to their physiological functions, a cornerstone of biomedical investigation. Integrative Biology: This area emphasizes connecting molecular and cellular-level processes to the whole-organism level, which aligns perfectly with the job's dual requirements. Cell and Molecular Biology: Candidates from this field are also highly valued, provided they can demonstrate significant experience and a strong background in whole-organism anatomy and zoology to effectively teach those courses. Ultimately, the key is not just the name of the degree, but the candidate's demonstrated ability to teach classical zoology and anatomy while running a contemporary, hands-on biomedical research program for undergraduates. Minimum of 1 year experience Preferred Skills Pedagogical Skills: A demonstrated ability to effectively teach and engage undergraduate students in both lecture and laboratory settings. This includes curriculum development, lesson planning, and creating meaningful assessments. Mentorship: The ability to guide, train, and inspire undergraduate students within a research environment, fostering their development as young scientists. Grant Writing: A strong skill in writing clear, persuasive, and successful grant proposals to secure funding from agencies like the NIH, NSF, or private foundations. Communication: Excellent written and oral communication skills are essential for publishing research in peer-reviewed journals, presenting at scientific conferences, and delivering clear lectures. Laboratory Management: Competence in running a research lab, which includes overseeing lab safety, managing a budget, ordering supplies, and maintaining equipment. Interpersonal Skills: The ability to collaborate effectively with other faculty, staff, and students, contributing to a positive and productive departmental community. Preferred Proficiencies Biomedical Laboratory Techniques: Proficiency in a range of modern laboratory techniques relevant to biomedical research. This could include, but isn't limited to: Molecular biology techniques (PCR, qPCR, DNA/RNA extraction, electrophoresis). Aseptic cell culture techniques. Advanced microscopy (light, fluorescent, confocal). Dissection techniques for both vertebrate and invertebrate organisms. Protein analysis (Western blotting, ELISA). Data Analysis Software: Proficiency with statistical software (e.g., R, SPSS, Prism) and other relevant bioinformatics tools for analyzing research data. Instructional Technology: Competence in using Learning Management Systems (LMS) like Canvas or Blackboard and other digital tools to enhance teaching. Preferred Knowledge Subject Matter Expertise: A deep and comprehensive understanding of Human Anatomy , Comparative Anatomy , and Zoology (both invertebrate and vertebrate). Research Design: Thorough knowledge of the scientific method, experimental design, and the ethical considerations involved in conducting biomedical research. Scientific Literature: A strong grasp of the current and foundational literature in the candidate's specific field of biomedical research. Funding Landscape: Knowledge of major external funding agencies, their specific missions, and their grant application cycles. Laboratory Safety: In-depth knowledge of safety protocols and regulations (e.g., OSHA, biosafety levels) necessary for maintaining a safe research and teaching environment. Langston University is an equal opportunity employer. Our hiring decisions are based on merit, qualifications, and institutional needs, ensuring equal opportunities for all individuals, regardless of race, gender, age, disability, or other protected characteristics. We welcome qualified applicants who share our commitment to academic excellence, community engagement, and fostering a vibrant campus culture. For more information, please contact luhumanresources@langston.edu. You may also be interested in School of Arts & Sciences/Biology Department Adjunct Instructor-1841 Langston VP ACADEMIC AFFAIRS (LU) Student Engagement and Leadership Coordinator-1793 Langston DEAN OF STUDENTS (LU) Nursing Instructor/Assistant Professor (Langston Campus)-1111 Langston VP ACADEMIC AFFAIRS (LU) Apply Now! First Name (required) e707c1dd Last Name (required) b4584e32 Email (required) cdd9052e Apply Please wait... Done Not You? By submitting this form, you consent to receive text messages related to available jobs and information about employment within the OSU/A&M System. Message & data rates may apply. Message frequency varies. Unsubscribe at any time by replying STOP or clicking the unsubscribe link (where available). By submitting your information, you acknowledge that you have read and agree to all system campus privacy policies. Thank you Candidate Details This website uses cookies. We use cookies to personalize content such as job recommendations and to analyze our traffic. You consent to our cookies if you click \"I Accept\". If you click on \"I Do Not Accept\", then we will not use cookies, which may impact your user experience. You can change your settings by clicking on the Settings link on the top right of the device. We will never sell your data to third parties. Review your institution's privacy policy below: OSU's privacy notice Langston University's privacy notice Northeastern Oklahoma A&M's privacy notice Connors State College privacy notice Manage Consent Preferences Strictly Necessary Cookies These cookies are necessary for the website to function and cannot be switched off in our systems. They are usually only set in response to actions made by you which amount to a request for services, such as setting your privacy preferences, logging in or filling in forms. You can set your browser to block or alert you about these cookies, but some parts of the site will not then work. Performance Cookies Performance Cookies: These cookies allow us to count visits and traffic sources so we can measure and improve the performance of our site. They help us to know which pages are the most and least popular and see how visitors move around the site. If you do not allow these cookie we will not know when you have visited our site, and will not be able to monitor its performance. Manage Cookies I do not accept I accept Save"
